<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Systema Naturae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1735">1735</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Systema Naturae is the work in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Carl Linnaeus</a> set out his classification of nature into the three kingdoms of animals, plants and minerals, divided into classes, orders, genera and species. First published at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> in 1735 as a short folio, it grew through many editions, and its tenth edition of 1758 is the formal starting point of zoological nomenclature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/systema-naturae.jpg" alt="Systema Naturae" width="320" height="517" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Linnaeus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linnaeus1758-title-page.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Carl Linnaeus</a> published the first edition of Systema Naturae in 1735 during his stay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. As was customary for scientific literature of the time, it was written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. The binomial system of naming was partly developed by the Bauhin brothers, Gaspard and Johann, but Linnaeus was the first to use it consistently throughout a book. He had already applied two-part names to plants in Species Plantarum in 1753, and in the tenth edition of 1758 he introduced them for animals.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Linnaeus</a> kept publishing new and larger editions because the work was popular and specimens were sent to him from around the world. It grew from 11 very large pages in 1735 to 2,400 pages in the twelfth edition of 1766 to 1768, the last under his authorship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work divides nature into the animal, plant and mineral kingdoms, using five levels: kingdom, class, order, genus and species. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Linnaeus</a> saw species and genera as given by God and the three higher levels as constructs meant to be easy to remember. In the tenth edition animals fall into six classes: Mammalia, Aves, Amphibia, Pisces, Insecta and Vermes. Whales, classed as fishes in the first edition, were moved among the mammals in 1758. Linnaeus was one of the first scientists to place humans among the primates, under the name Anthropomorpha.</p><p>He divided Homo sapiens into four varieties corresponding to the four known continents and four temperaments. The tenth edition added behavioural and cultural traits to these descriptions, which the Linnean Society recognises as having cemented colonial stereotypes and provided one of the foundations of scientific racism. His plant classes, the Sexual System, grouped species by the number of stamens. It was convenient, but in his view artificial. The work lists only about 10,000 species, about 6,000 of them plants and 4,236 animals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Linnaeus</a>'s health declined in the early 1770s, Johan Andreas Murray issued the plant section separately in 1774 as the Systema Vegetabilium, confusingly labelled the thirteenth edition. A thirteenth edition of the whole work appeared in parts between 1788 and 1793 under Johann Friedrich Gmelin, which zoologists have commonly treated as the last of the series.</p><p>The tenth edition is regarded as the starting point of zoological nomenclature, with one exception in zoology, Carl Clerck's monograph on Swedish spiders of 1757, whose names take priority. By the end of the 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Linnaeus</a>'s system had effectively become the standard for biological classification. Only in the animal kingdom is his higher taxonomy still recognisable, and his classification of minerals has long since fallen out of use. The old question whether something is animal, vegetable or mineral still echoes his three kingdoms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Systema Naturae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Swedish scientists included Carl Linnaeus, whose <b>Systema Naturae</b> (1735) founded modern biological classification, and Anders Celsius, and the will of Alfred Nobel created the Nobel Prizes, first awarded in 1901.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
